<section class="section split-panel" id="origins" aria-labelledby="origins-heading">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">Origins</p>
    <h2 id="origins-heading">What the reserve was built from</h2>
    <p>
      The reserve was not new land in 2013. According to the NTCA brief note and the Tiger Conservation Plan, its
      core joined four existing protected areas, and its buffer took in surrounding forests of Kota, Chittorgarh and
      Jhalawar (later also Bundi). These forests had been hunting reserves of the Kota, Bundi, Chittorgarh and
      Jhalawar princely states.
    </p>
    <p>
      In 2023 the Bhainsrodgarh area was added to the core. The updated TCP describes this as the addition of the
      Bhainsrodgarh Wildlife Sanctuary.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="scope-list" aria-label="Protected areas combined into MHTR">
    <div><strong>Darrah Wildlife Sanctuary</strong><span>Declared 7 November 1955 (Rajasthan Wild Animals and Birds Protection Act, 1951).</span></div>
    <div><strong>Jawahar Sagar Wildlife Sanctuary</strong><span>Declared 9 October 1975.</span></div>
    <div><strong>National Chambal Sanctuary (part)</strong><span>Declared 3 September 1983; the stretch from Garadia Mahadev to Jawahar Sagar dam went into the core.</span></div>
    <div><strong>Mukundara Hills National Park</strong><span>The TCP gives both 2007 and 2012 as its notification year; the original notification has not yet been traced for this site.</span></div>
    <div><strong>Bhainsrodgarh area (2023)</strong><span>Added to the core by the 5 October 2023 notification.</span></div>
  </div>
</section>
