<section class="section split-panel" aria-labelledby="esz-heading">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">Eco-Sensitive Zone</p>
    <h2 id="esz-heading">Two variable-width regulatory zones</h2>
    <p>
      The 2020 Gazette describes an ESZ extending from zero to one kilometre around the 2013 reserve and
      covering 248.70 sq km. Zero extent occurs in directions where the notification records
      co-termination with the tiger-reserve buffer. The Gazette also provides boundary descriptions,
      coordinates, maps and a village list; in a few sectors its own coordinates and map put the outer
      limit well over one kilometre out. Near Rawatbhata, where Annexure-I gives a width of zero "due to
      Bhainsroadgarh Sanctuary on the other side", the outer limit lies, by our measurement, about 5.4 km beyond the
      Forest Department's reserve file and 5.7 km beyond the Gazette's own reserve coordinates (Table A). There it runs
      along the edge of Bhainsrorgarh Wildlife Sanctuary, so the zone fills all the land between the reserve and the
      sanctuary.
    </p>
    <p>
      Bhainsrorgarh Wildlife Sanctuary has its own ESZ, notified in 2021: 304.70 sq km, zero to 9.6 km wide.
      The sanctuary became MHTR core in 2023, so the reserve now has two ESZ notifications around different
      parts of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The ESZ does not prohibit every activity. The notification and Zonal Master Plan distinguish
      prohibited, regulated and promoted activities. Site-specific permissions remain the responsibility
      of the competent government authorities.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="scope-list" aria-label="ESZ interpretation">
    <div><strong>248.70 sq km</strong><span>Notified MHTR ESZ area (2020).</span></div>
    <div><strong>0–1 km</strong><span>Variable outward extent stated in the 2020 Gazette.</span></div>
