    <div><strong>Separate legal layer</strong><span>Not part of the core-plus-buffer total.</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="kml-heading">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">GIS Interpretation</p>
    <h2 id="kml-heading">How the Department's ESZ files should be used</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid-2">
    <article class="card">
      <h3>Appropriate use</h3>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Show a dated ESZ outer-limit or context overlay.</li>
        <li>Retain the source label “Rajasthan Forest Department, ESZ KML as on 01.06.2026”.</li>
        <li>Read the geometry with the 2020 Gazette and 2023 Zonal Master Plan.</li>
        <li>Use the notification schedules and revenue records for legal interpretation.</li>
        <li>For a single outline of the reserve and both ESZs, use the joined <a href="/gis-maps/#boundary-files">boundary files</a>, which state their gaps.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>Incorrect use</h3>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Do not replace the reserve or Critical Tiger Habitat polygon with the ESZ KML.</li>
        <li>Do not colour the complete enclosed footprint as 248.70 sq km of ESZ.</li>
        <li>Do not describe a KML calculation as a notified legal area.</li>
        <li>Do not fill the 3.53 sq km enclave in the MHTR file, but do not treat it as settled either. The file and the Gazette's map (Annexure-IIB) leave it outside both the reserve and the zone, yet the Gazette's village list (Annexure-IV, S.No. 52) names Jawara Khurd, whose listed point lies inside the enclave, as an ESZ village. Only the Department can say which is meant.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="mapping-gaps" aria-labelledby="gaps-heading">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Mapping Gaps</p>
    <h2 id="gaps-heading">What the public map files cannot show</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      We joined the Forest Department's map files into one outline (see the <a href="/gis-maps/#boundary-files">GIS
      boundary files</a>). These are the gaps and inconsistencies we found, checked on 23 October 2026. Each is a
      question the Department can answer.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="info-grid">
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">About {{ boundaryAreas.stats.not_mapped_notified_km2 | round }} sq km</span>
      <h3>Part of the 2023 reserve has no outline of its own</h3>
      <p>
        No public file outlines the 21 revenue villages added to the core (80.45 sq km): the Bhainsrorgarh
        sanctuary file leaves nine unnamed enclaves, and some village land may lie inside its outline. The Kota and
        Bundi files draw only whole blocks, not the buffer parts of Bardkalan Kishor Sagar and Sakatpura in Kota or of
        Karondi, Rampuriya and Dhaneshwar in Bundi (added by area or compartment). No file reaches Ambarani-A
        (3.54 sq km) or the National Chambal buffer piece (23.85 sq km). Akelgarh (27.84 sq km) is listed without a
        part mark; 7.75 sq km of the block has been core since 2013, and the Kota file draws the rest at about
        32 sq km, close to but not the notified area.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Core and buffer</span>
      <h3>No file separates core from buffer</h3>
      <p>
        Neither the 2013 nor the 2023 split is drawn in any public file. Our approximation of the 2013 core from the
        park and sanctuary files misassigns about 26 sq km between core and buffer.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Reserve file</span>
      <h3>The reserve file still draws 2013</h3>
      <p>
        "Mukundara Tiger Reserve.kmz" is dated 24 February 2025 on the server, but its attributes give the 2013 area
        (759.99 sq km notified, 744.85 digitised) and it draws only the 2013 reserve.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Bhainsrorgarh</span>
      <h3>Three different sanctuary areas</h3>
      <p>
        The sanctuary is 201.4 sq km in the Department's map file attribute and 275.45 sq km in the 2021 ESZ
        notification; the file itself draws 206.01 sq km. By our sum of its hectare rows, the 2023 notification adds 12
        forest blocks of the Bhainsrorgarh area, 193.09 sq km, to the core.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">2020 ESZ</span>
      <h3>Wider than stated, and may overlap the 2023 buffer</h3>
      <p>
        The Gazette states 0–1 km, but its own coordinates and map are wider in a few sectors. It was drawn around
        the 2013 reserve and covers about 29 sq km of the Bardkalan Kishor Sagar block, 11 of Akelgarh and 8 of
        Karondi. The 2023 notification adds part of each block to the buffer without a map, so no public file
        shows how much land is now both ESZ and buffer.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Small mismatches</span>
      <h3>An enclosed pocket and a short block</h3>
      <p>
        A 6.2 sq km area around Daulatpura is closed off where the Bhainsrorgarh file meets the reserve file, but no
        reserve file includes it, and the Tiger Conservation Plan's list of the 21 villages added in 2023 does not
        name it. The 2020 ESZ file covers 6.1 sq km of it. The Kota file draws Borabas-B at
        0.50 sq km against 1.38 sq km notified.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">5 October 2023 notification</span>
      <h3>Village areas do not match their total</h3>
      <p>
        The notification's 24 Rawatbhata village areas add to 8,695.28 ha, but its printed total is 8,194.28 ha, 501 ha
        less. Three cells under the signature stamp are read from the Plan's Annexure-2, and the gap does not depend
        on them. The 690.71 sq km core follows the printed total; by the rows it would be 695.72 sq km. The Tiger
        Conservation Plan repeats the printed figure.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Forest Department MHTR page</span>
      <h3>Missing and broken links</h3>
      <p>
        On the Department's MHTR page (seen 23 October 2026; website footer: last update 18 October 2026), the rows for Settlement of Rights, Boundary
        Pillars and Khasra have no link, and the Forest Area link opens a Jawai Bandh page that returns “404”.
      </p>
      <a href="https://forest.rajasthan.gov.in/content/raj/forest/en/aboutus/departmental-wings/wildlife/details-of-protected-area-/mukundara-hills-tiger-reserve.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open the Department's MHTR page</a>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Not established</span>
      <h3>What we could not check</h3>
      <p>
        This page does not establish whether any area has been changed or denotified since 5 October 2023. We found
        no later notification, but did not search every gazette, and could not reach archived copies of older
        Department pages.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <p class="source-note">
    Areas are measured on the WGS84 ellipsoid from the files listed on the <a href="/gis-maps/#official-files">GIS
    page</a>; the full table of measured and notified areas is there too. See the
